WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.760
Whenever you watch a Disney movie,&nbsp;
you can be sure there will be an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.920
Easter egg or 10 hidden within it. It's&nbsp;
no surprise, then, that Raya and the Last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.600
Dragon continues this proud tradition,&nbsp;
and we're here to help you spot them.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.880
Although Pua the pig is a whole lot&nbsp;
cuter, as far as animal sidekicks go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.880
Heihei the rooster is the creature who accompanies&nbsp;
Moana on her adventure in her 2016 movie. Indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.760
the not-so-bright bird winds up traveling with her&nbsp;
across the sea after accidentally stowing away on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.640
her boat. He might be dim-witted, but he still&nbsp;
manages to get into all sorts of shenanigans.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.440
Alan Tudyk, the actor who provided Heihei's voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.960
also voices Raya's pet and steed, Tuk Tuk.&nbsp;
Possibly as a nod to Tudyk's previous role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.480
Heihei shows up very briefly in&nbsp;
Talon's busy central marketplace.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.840
Tuk Tuk is Raya's constant and adorable&nbsp;
companion and he's also her steed:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.600
We most frequently see him rolling&nbsp;
underneath Raya like a living unicycle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.960
This isn't just a neat idea,&nbsp;
though — it's a cultural reference.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.040
Tuk-tuks are a type of vehicle commonly&nbsp;
seen in many Southeast Asian countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.640
also called an auto rickshaw. They&nbsp;
are open-air, have three wheels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.200
and get their name from the sound they make.&nbsp;
Tuk-tuks originated in Japan in the 1930s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.240
and went on to become popular throughout Southeast&nbsp;
Asia. Raya's Tuk Tuk might not be motorized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.560
but he does make a slightly automotive noise&nbsp;
as he rolls along the roads of Kumandra.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.320
As Raya walks through Talon's busy streets, a&nbsp;
number of shots reveal a dog that looks somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:30.160
like a Xoloitzcuintli, also called Xolos, the&nbsp;
type of dog portrayed in Disney's Coco. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.360
this pooch might just be a reference to Miguel's&nbsp;
beloved pet, Dante. Though the dog isn't exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:39.840
a visual match, he could also be an Easter&nbsp;
egg in reference to Southeast Asian culture.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.160
In keeping with the meticulous design of the&nbsp;
film, the Talon dog is most likely to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.320
Southeast Asian village dog. Village dogs,&nbsp;
which are found throughout Asia, are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.120
quite domesticated, and they are not purebreds.&nbsp;
They are genetically diverse, and thought to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.920
be descendants of the original domesticated&nbsp;
dogs of Central Asia from 15,000 years ago.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.160
In an interview with The Hollywood Reporter, Lao&nbsp;
visual anthropologist Steve Arounsack discussed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:09.760
several of Raya's cultural details, including&nbsp;
what inspired Sisu's design: the legendary Naga.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:14.720
In Hinduism, Buddhism, and Jainism, Naga&nbsp;
are, according to Encyclopedia Britannica,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.520
"[...]a member of a class of mythical&nbsp;
semidivine beings, half human and half cobra."

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.760
They have the ability to shapeshift and can be&nbsp;
good or bad, though they're usually good. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.640
also live near and in bodies of water. One big&nbsp;
difference between the Naga and Raya's dragons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.480
though, is that Naga have no legs.&nbsp;
Design-wise, this would've made it very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.120
hard to depict Sisu holding the magical Dragon&nbsp;
Gem, so the design of the dragons was tweaked.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.440
Depictions of Naga appear throughout the&nbsp;
lands of Kumandra. This reflects real life:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.320
Naga have been carved into doors, adorned canopy&nbsp;
beds, and guarded gates in the form of enormous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.960
statues. Basically, they're revered both in&nbsp;
the film and in actual Southeast Asian culture.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.720
Raya's sword, which belonged to her father and&nbsp;
which she uses after he is turned to stone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:02.000
has a dragon and gem for a hilt. This repeated&nbsp;
motif is a depiction of Sisu, the one being who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.720
binds the lands of Kumandra together, even as&nbsp;
they drift apart. Additionally, the dragons who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.440
were turned to stone 500 years before the story&nbsp;
begins dot the landscape: When passing through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.240
a valley full of the dormant creatures, Namaari&nbsp;
and her entourage make sure to show them respect.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:21.440
But literal depictions of dragons aren't the only&nbsp;
way Sisu and her magical siblings are referenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.080
in Raya and the Last Dragon – water symbols&nbsp;
are everywhere in the film. One example is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:31.307
raindrop motif on Raya's cape. The dragons' powers&nbsp;
over water are a huge part of their magic — there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.307 --> 00:03:35.520
is no life without water, of course, and&nbsp;
rain is a big part of that relationship.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.280
After the Dragon Gem is shattered, the&nbsp;
freshly-empowered Druun attack Kumandra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.000
with terrifying strength. It takes Raya six&nbsp;
long and arduous years, armed with a potent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.120
shard of the Dragon Gem, to search through all&nbsp;
of Kumandra's rivers and eventually find Sisu.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.520
Raya grasps the exhaustive nature of her&nbsp;
search as well as the audience does: She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.400
comments on how many tributaries the main river of&nbsp;
Kumandra has when looking at a map of her world.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.280
Given that the main inspiration for that key&nbsp;
body of water is the real-world Mekong River,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:10.560
it's no wonder it took her so much time:&nbsp;
The Mekong is about 2,700 miles long!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.040
This mighty river flows through and between the&nbsp;
Southeast Asian countries of Laos, Thailand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.480
Myanmar, Cambodia, and Vietnam. The Mekong&nbsp;
is also associated with the legendary Naga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.800
particularly the mysterious phenomenon&nbsp;
known as the Naga fireballs. That makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.800
a Mekong-inspired river the perfect&nbsp;
setting for Raya's search for Sisu.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:35.360
Much of the architecture of Kumandra resembles&nbsp;
Cambodia's iconic Angkor Wat temple complex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:39.680
especially in Fang. Inspiration taken&nbsp;
from the Indonesian rumah gadang style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.360
of house is also evident. Though the&nbsp;
term "Southeast Asia'' encompasses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.360
several countries, there are only five&nbsp;
in Kumandra, and so the filmmakers drew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.200
inspiration from more than one when&nbsp;
developing each land's distinct look.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.760
Consider the gorgeous, chaotic,&nbsp;
totally-filled-with-thieves floating market of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.640
Talon. This unique environment draws inspiration&nbsp;
from two distinct Southeast Asian settings: The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:05.680
Vietnamese water markets of the Mekong River, and&nbsp;
the luminous night markets of Thailand and Laos.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.160
Such blended cultural details are also apparent&nbsp;
in the types of weapons each land prefers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.160
the type of clothing everyone wears, and&nbsp;
even people's jewelry. Raya, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.080
wears something very reminiscent of the&nbsp;
Filipino salakot. This combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:22.080
various Southeast Asian cultures allows for&nbsp;
more of them to be showcased in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.280
while firmly establishing the&nbsp;
fictional style of each land.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.400
Throughout most of Raya and the Last Dragon,&nbsp;
the audience sees Raya as the good princess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:34.720
and Namaari as the bad one — though of course,&nbsp;
the film's climax reveals they have far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.800
in common than they think. One thing that is very&nbsp;
different, though, is their approach to fighting.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.520
When we first meet Raya, she is trying to get into&nbsp;
the temple in Heart that houses the Dragon Gem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.080
In order to open a secret stone doorway, she&nbsp;
pulls out two bamboo sticks and puts them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:52.240
in holes in the stone to turn and unlock&nbsp;
it. Those sticks are used in the Filipino&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:58.160
martial arts form Arnis, which is also known&nbsp;
as Kali and Eskrima. Arnis is mainly defensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:02.320
and involves fighting with sticks, swords,&nbsp;
and daggers. It is a combination of various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:06.880
fighting forms developed over thousands&nbsp;
of years. Raya also uses Pencak silat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:11.760
which combines the art of movement, self-defense,&nbsp;
physicality, and spirituality. Furthermore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.880
Chief Benja's sword, which Raya wields after her&nbsp;
father is turned to stone, is an Indonesian kris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:21.600
which has a wavy blade and is considered to&nbsp;
be a spiritual object as well as a weapon.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:27.120
In contrast, Namaari uses Muay Thai and Krabi&nbsp;
krabong. Muay Thai is somewhat similar to boxing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.760
while Krabi krabong uses weapons. Both&nbsp;
fighting styles originated in Thailand.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:38.320
As Raya, Sisu, and their friends travel across&nbsp;
Kumandra, Sisu frequently suggests giving gifts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:42.640
as a way of getting their merry band into the good&nbsp;
graces of the people they're visiting. The giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:46.880
of gifts is tradition in all of Asia, but there&nbsp;
are different customs in different countries.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.800
Another important cultural norm is the&nbsp;
use of a hand gesture to greet others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.560
This is given a twist in Kumandra,&nbsp;
as the characters make a gem shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:58.480
with their hands when meeting each other. As&nbsp;
Steve Arounsack told The Hollywood Reporter,

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:02.000
"That's a creative take on the common&nbsp;
greetings you'll see in the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.480
[Southeast Asian] region, where you have&nbsp;
clasped hands. It's called a nop in Lao&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:10.400
and a wai in Thai, but it's the same&nbsp;
theme of bringing your hands together."

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.920
In Kumandra, the height of the gesture&nbsp;
indicates the amount of respect given:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.760
Sisu receives the highest greetings of all,&nbsp;
even from Noi and her mischievous pack of Ongis.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.800
After Raya and Sisu meet 12-year-old Boun,&nbsp;
he winds up going with them on their quest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.440
for the remaining pieces of the Dragon Gem. This&nbsp;
allows the party to travel aboard his boat — or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:34.720
as he calls it, his "Shrimporium." Boun ferries&nbsp;
them from land to land along Kumandra's river,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:39.760
but he doesn't do it for free — Raya has to offer&nbsp;
him some of her jade as payment. As they travel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:42.480
things become increasingly perilous for the gang.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:46.640
Ferrymen frequently function as symbols&nbsp;
in literature and myth. Consider Charon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:50.800
a figure from Greek mythology who ferries&nbsp;
the dead across the River Styx to Hades — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:55.840
dangerous journey on a good day. The dead need&nbsp;
to give Charon one coin as fare for safe passage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:00.880
much like Raya has to bribe Boun with jade.&nbsp;
Likewise, at the climax of the film, all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:06.320
characters except Namaari wind up turned to stone,&nbsp;
a form of death that is undone once Namaari is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:10.480
able to put the Dragon Gem back together and&nbsp;
defeat the Druun. The Gem is used as a form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.640
of payment — albeit, one that brings the dead&nbsp;
back over the border to the land of the living.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:20.320
Savvy Disney fans know that the studio loves&nbsp;
hiding Mickeys all over the place. There are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:24.480
in fact, multiple books and websites&nbsp;
dedicated to finding every "hidden Mickey"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:28.240
possible. A hidden Mickey isn't an actual&nbsp;
drawing of Mickey Mouse placed somewhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:32.320
as in a Where's Waldo? puzzle, however.&nbsp;
It's a simple representation of Mickey's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.280
head — one larger circle with&nbsp;
two smaller circles on it.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:40.000
In a recent interview with Malaysian website&nbsp;
Says, directors Don Hall and Carlos López&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.920
Estrada stated that there are hidden&nbsp;
Mickeys in the land of Talon, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:47.440
"tiny little" ones. If we're correct&nbsp;
about one of those "tiny little" ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:52.320
then the three-dotted patterns on Talon&nbsp;
chief Dang Hu's hat certainly count.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:55.440
Other possible hidden Mickeys can&nbsp;
be seen to the right of the kitchen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.000
Raya and her father enter early in the film,&nbsp;
and on the larger of Raya's leather arm bands.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:05.280
It's not a huge secret that Disney sometimes&nbsp;
recycles animation in its film. But sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.320
a scene is less recycled than it is deliberately&nbsp;
echoing another Disney property. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:14.080
the shipwreck scene in Raya and the&nbsp;
Last Dragon echoes the one in Frozen II.

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:18.160
In Frozen II, Anna and Elsa find a shipwreck&nbsp;
which turns out to be the ship on which their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:22.880
parents perished. In what might be a wry&nbsp;
nod to that scene, when Raya and Tuk Tuk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:26.880
come to the end of their search for Sisu,&nbsp;
they find a wrecked ship. Raya comments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:30.560
"Six years of searching and we end up at a&nbsp;
literal shipwreck." Is the film making fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:35.040
of Anna and Elsa's sad discovery? Probably&nbsp;
not. But pointing out such a similar scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.760
even though it has a different outcome, is no&nbsp;
doubt a wink to the Easter egg-savvy Disney fan.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:44.080
The Hollywood Reporter quoted director&nbsp;
Don Hall as saying that some of Raya's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:47.360
"swagger" is pulled from Guardians&nbsp;
of the Galaxy's Star-Lord.

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:47.860
"Who?"

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:51.120
"Star-Lord. Legendary outlaw? C'mon."

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.000
That's definitely not the&nbsp;
only movie Raya references.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.840
When Tong decides to throw in his&nbsp;
lot with Raya and Sisu, he says,

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:01.280
"I wish to join this fellowship of buttkickery."

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:05.120
We're counting this as a shout-out to&nbsp;
another fantasy epic, The Lord of the Rings:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.200
The Fellowship of the Ring. Raya even wears&nbsp;
her childhood hair ring on a necklace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:11.760
after all, just like Frodo does with the One Ring!

